  "label": "TMUA 2020 · Paper 1 · Question 6",
  "lead": "<img class=\"source-question\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge question\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"TMUA 2020 · Paper 1 · Question 6 — Find the maximum of 1/(5^(2x)-4·5^x+7).\">",
  "options": [
    "A",
    "B",
    "C",
    "D",
    "E",
    "F"
  ],
  "correct": "C",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Rewrite the denominator",
      "body": "<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>5</mn><mi>x</mi></msup></mrow></math>. Use the power rules to write the denominator as a quadratic in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>.</p>",
      "recap": "A positive number raised to a real power stays positive.",
      "pitfall": "Doubling an exponent is different from doubling the value.",
      "pause": "Write the quadratic and note which values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> are possible.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l05",
          "reminder": "A positive number raised to a real power stays positive."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Make the denominator as small as possible",
      "body": "<p>Complete the square in the denominator. Check that it stays positive, then find its smallest possible value.</p>",
      "recap": "For a positive denominator, a smaller denominator makes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mtext>denominator</mtext></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> larger.",
      "pitfall": "Check that the required <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> can really equal <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>5</mn><mi>x</mi></msup></mrow></math>.",
      "pause": "Use the smallest possible denominator to find the maximum of the fraction.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l07",
          "reminder": "For a positive denominator, a smaller denominator makes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mtext>denominator</mtext></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> larger."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p>Put <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>5</mn><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>.</p><p>The denominator is at least 3 and is positive for every allowed input. Taking reciprocals therefore gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. Equality requires <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, which is possible at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow></msub><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p>Thus the bound is attained and the maximum is <strong><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, option C.</strong> The positivity check is essential: reciprocal inequalities cannot be used without controlling the denominator’s sign.</p>",
